+# Flight 93 911 call: comparison of two published transcripts
+
+2026-11-14 UTC · AUD016-A1. The [preceding custody review](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/5c4e405993fd47d3fdf329e73c5673c91d0dc4ab/investigation/broader-evidence/telephone-felt-public-transcript-2026-11-14/README.md) did not compare the transcripts. This follow-up compares selected text from the released FBI transcript and the National Park Service (NPS) presentation. It does not compare either with the audio.
+
+## Source observations
+
+- The released [FBI transcript, physical PDF page 91](https://archive.org/download/911datasets/NARA_9-11_Commission_Records_Released_Jan_14_2009.zip/NARA_9-11_Commission_Records_Released_Jan_14_2009/911%20NARA/Team%207%20Files/Team%207%20Box%2012/T7%20B12%20DOJ%20Doc%20Req%2035-13%20Packet%204%20Fdr-%20Entire%20Contents-%20Response%20Letters-%20Reports%20403.pdf#page=91), extracted line 19, spells the caller's third statement **“Highjacking in progress!”** The [NPS presentation](https://www.nps.gov/flni/learn/historyculture/phone-calls-from-flight-93.htm) spells the corresponding statement **“Hijacking in progress!”** Earlier occurrences are not all changed. Both source wordings remain visible in the linked records. The FBI page was visually checked.
+- The FBI transcript uses `(UI)` in four places across physical pages 91–92; NPS uses `(unintelligible)` in four corresponding positions in the dialogue examined. These are the source labels; the audio and transcription instructions were not available to verify the underlying sounds. [FBI page 91](https://archive.org/download/911datasets/NARA_9-11_Commission_Records_Released_Jan_14_2009.zip/NARA_9-11_Commission_Records_Released_Jan_14_2009/911%20NARA/Team%207%20Files/Team%207%20Box%2012/T7%20B12%20DOJ%20Doc%20Req%2035-13%20Packet%204%20Fdr-%20Entire%20Contents-%20Response%20Letters-%20Reports%20403.pdf#page=91) (extracted lines 33–34), [page 92](https://archive.org/download/911datasets/NARA_9-11_Commission_Records_Released_Jan_14_2009.zip/NARA_9-11_Commission_Records_Released_Jan_14_2009/911%20NARA/Team%207%20Files/Team%207%20Box%2012/T7%20B12%20DOJ%20Doc%20Req%2035-13%20Packet%204%20Fdr-%20Entire%20Contents-%20Response%20Letters-%20Reports%20403.pdf#page=92) (lines 31, 33–39), and [NPS](https://www.nps.gov/flni/learn/historyculture/phone-calls-from-flight-93.htm).
+- NPS explicitly substitutes redaction markers for portions of a caller-identification exchange. That sensitive exchange is excluded from this comparison and public report. Its treatment cannot be used to assess differences in the omitted content.
+
+## Reproducible comparison and limits
+
+The accompanying `COMPARE.py` checks the pinned source hashes and extracts FBI PDF page 91 lines 12–38 and page 92 lines 12–16 and 24–40. It extracts the corresponding NPS dialogue, omitting the intervening identifying exchange on both sides. It lowercases text, discards punctuation and speaker labels, maps `(UI)` to `unintelligible` **only for this test**, and aligns the resulting words and digits in order. The retained sources preserve their original forms. On these rules, **272 of 273 tokens** align; the remaining token is the spelling difference above. This is a normalized text comparison, not a word-for-word or whole-document equivalence test. Punctuation, capitalization, labels, the omitted exchange, formatting, and any OCR errors are outside the agreement claim.
+
+The source PDF is a released scan; the test uses text extraction, with the spelling and first `(UI)` checked against the page image and lower page 92 checked visually. A separate review of all pixels and the original audio has not been performed. NPS attributes the presentation to an FBI transcript; the comparison is consistent with closely related text, but does not independently establish which FBI copy NPS used or its editing history. NPS and FBI text are not independent recordings.
+
+The spelling and marker differences could arise during transcription, reproduction or presentation; **those explanations are untested**. They do not establish an altered recording or intent. The actual tape or authenticated copy, transcription history, and dispatch and carrier records remain necessary to test the accuracy, timing and custody questions. The reported **9:58 EDT / 70 seconds** remains an NPS claim, not an independent measurement by this review.
+
+## Provenance and preservation
+
+The retained [source pins](SOURCE-PINS.json) include the 2009 Commission release's packet hash and the NPS captured-page hash and access dates. Raw originals remain in the research workspace; no raw scan, contact data or private correspondence is published. The previous report remains unchanged; `TEXT-CHANGES.diff` records this new additive note against an empty predecessor. The verifier tests bytes and the specified normalized comparison, not the historical truth of either transcript.
